# Layer layout (repository → service → delivery)

Readability goal: **open any file once and know its job** — same structural habits as `references/be-suretyship/internal/auth/`, adapted for WiChat (gRPC MS, HTTP gateway, `wi-shared/exception` + `wi-shared/response`). Comment and spacing detail: `wichat-go-style.mdc`.

## Principles

- **Thin delivery** — bind/parse, call service, write response. No business rules.
- **Service owns domain** — validation, orchestration, map repo errors → `exception`.
- **Dumb repository** — I/O only; plain `error`; no `exception` import.
- **Interface at the boundary** — exported `Xxx` interface, unexported `xxx` struct, `NewXxx(...) Xxx`.
- **Value receivers** on impl methods unless the receiver must mutate shared state (match suretyship default).
- **Inject `logger.Logger`** (`wi-shared/infra/logger`) in service, delivery, and repository when I/O errors matter.

## Folder layout (per domain)

```text
backend/ms-auth/internal/auth/
  repository/          # postgres_*.go, redis_*.go
  service/             # service.go (interface + impl)
  delivery/grpc/       # microservice handlers

backend/gateway/internal/auth/
  delivery/http/       # public REST only
```

Payload/DTO structs: `internal/auth/model` or colocated with `service` when small.

## Repository

Pattern (one backing store per file is fine):

```go
package repository

type PgRepository interface {
    FindUserByID(ctx context.Context, id string) (*User, error)
}

type pgRepository struct {
    db  *sql.DB
    log logger.Logger // optional
}

func NewPgRepository(db *sql.DB, log logger.Logger) PgRepository {
    return pgRepository{db: db, log: log}
}

func (r pgRepository) FindUserByID(ctx context.Context, id string) (*User, error) {
    // I/O only — no business branching beyond mapping driver errors
}

func (r pgRepository) userTable() string { return "auth.users" } // private helpers at bottom
```

Checklist:

- `context.Context` first on every method.
- Name interfaces by store: `PgRepository`, `RedisRepository`.
- Sentinel errors OK: `var ErrNotFound = errors.New("...")` — service maps to `exception`.
- No i18n, no HTTP/gRPC, no `wi-shared/exception`.

## Service

Package name **`service`** (not `usecase`). Deps are **repository interfaces** (and config/logger), injected via `NewService`.

```go
package service

type Service interface {
    Login(ctx context.Context, in *model.LoginInput) (*model.LoginResult, error)
}

type service struct {
    users repository.PgRepository
    log   logger.Logger
}

func NewService(users repository.PgRepository, log logger.Logger) Service {
    return service{users: users, log: log}
}

func (s service) Login(ctx context.Context, in *model.LoginInput) (*model.LoginResult, error) {
    // Validation
    if in.Email == "" {
        return nil, exception.InvalidInput(exception.MsgInvalidInput, map[string]any{"field": "email"})
    }

    // Repository
    u, err := s.users.FindByEmail(ctx, in.Email)
    if err != nil {
        if errors.Is(err, repository.ErrNotFound) {
            return nil, exception.Unauthenticated(exception.MsgInvalidCredentials, nil)
        }
        return nil, err // raw infra → delivery/grpc → 500
    }

    // Domain logic ...
    return &model.LoginResult{...}, nil
}
```

Checklist:

- Step comments: `// Validation`, `// Repository`, … — same order in every method (`wichat-go-style.mdc`).
- Return `exception.*` with **`MessageCode` consts** only (no hardcoded user-facing sentences).
- Constants / magic values at top of file in `const` blocks.
- Do not import `response/http` or `response/grpc` here.

## Delivery

### HTTP (gateway) — `delivery/http`

```go
type Handler interface {
    Login(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request)
}

type handler struct {
    svc service.Service
    log logger.Logger
}

func NewHandler(svc service.Service, log logger.Logger) Handler {
    return handler{svc: svc, log: log}
}

func (h handler) Login(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
    // Bind
    var in model.LoginInput
    if err := json.NewDecoder(r.Body).Decode(&in); err != nil {
        responsehttp.WriteError(w, r, exception.InvalidInput(exception.MsgInvalidInput, nil))
        return
    }

    // Service
    out, err := h.svc.Login(r.Context(), &in)
    if err != nil {
        responsehttp.WriteError(w, r, err, responsehttp.WithLogger(h.log))
        return
    }

    // Response
    responsehttp.WriteJSON(w, http.StatusOK, map[string]any{"data": out})
}
```

Downstream gRPC errors: `responsehttp.WriteErrorFromGRPC(w, r, err, ...)`.

### gRPC (microservice) — `delivery/grpc`

```go
func (s *AuthServer) Login(ctx context.Context, req *pb.LoginRequest) (*pb.LoginResponse, error) {
    // Bind (proto → model)
    // Service
    out, err := s.svc.Login(ctx, toLoginInput(req))
    if err != nil {
        return nil, responsegrpc.ToStatus(err)
    }
    // Response
    return toLoginPB(out), nil
}
```

Checklist:

- Same `// Bind` / `// Service` / `// Response` steps.
- Never `http.Error`, raw `status.Error` with driver text, or skip writers on errors.
- Cookies, headers, proto mapping stay in delivery — not in service.

## Anti-patterns

- SQL/Redis calls in handler.
- `exception` in repository.
- 200-line handlers or services — split by use case file only when interface is still one `Service`.
- Leaking `err.Error()` from bind/DB to JSON on 5xx.

